## Initial Q&A
### What is the stated mechanism of the scam where fake Santander bank cards are sent to customers, according to any official warnings or reports?
Answer: According to the search result, the scam involves criminals using "number spoofing" to send text messages that appear to be from Santander, warning of unusual account activity. Victims are then tricked into calling a number or clicking a link, where they are convinced to provide their online banking details and a One-Time Passcode (OTP), which the scammers use to empty their accounts. The source does not mention the physical mailing of fake bank cards.

Source URL: https://www.safecommunitiesportugal.com/cybercrimealerts/fraudulent-text-messages-in-the-name-of-santander/

### In which country or region has the scam involving the mailing of fake Santander bank cards been reported?
Answer: The scam involving fraudulent debit card use at Santander ATMs was reported in the United States, specifically in New York City, as the NYPD investigated incidents "at numerous Santander banks in Queens, Brooklyn and Staten Island."

Source URL: https://www.ny1.com/nyc/all-boroughs/news/2020/08/19/santander-bank-glitch-leads-to-atm-fraud

### What evidence exists, such as images of the fake cards or descriptions of the accompanying letters, to corroborate claims of a scam involving fake Santander bank cards?
Answer: The search result does describe a scam involving fake Santander debit cards, with the South Windsor police stating they had "been made aware of an ATM scam in which suspects are using Santander Bank ATM's to fraudulently withdraw cash using fake debit cards." However, this source describes an ATM-based fraud operation where suspects physically used fake or prepaid debit cards to withdraw cash at Santander ATMs across multiple states, rather than a scam involving Santander customers being mailed fake bank cards with accompanying letters. The search result does not contain images of fake cards or descriptions of letters that would directly corroborate the specific claim made by Nigel Howard Berry about customers being *sent* fake bank cards.

