## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that reported Santander-related scams during this period involved fraudulent text messages or ATM fraud, but no evidence was found confirming that Santander customers were physically sent fake bank cards in the mail. The sources describe criminals using number-spoofing for phishing texts or using fake cards at ATMs, not a scheme involving mailing counterfeit cards to customers.

The key missing information is any direct corroboration—such as police reports, official Santander warnings, or news articles—specifically stating that customers received unsolicited, physical, fake Santander bank cards. Without such evidence, the claim as stated remains unverified.

Based on the available record, there is insufficient information to confirm the claim. While various scams targeting Santander customers exist, none of the documented evidence supports the specific mechanism of customers being sent fake bank cards.

`not enough information`

### Verdict: NEI

### Justification
Fact-checking investigations found documented Santander-related scams during this period involving [fraudulent text messages](https://www.safecommunitiesportugal.com/cybercrimealerts/fraudulent-text-messages-in-the-name-of-santander/) and [ATM fraud using fake debit cards](https://nypost.com/2020/08/18/multi-state-santander-scam-sparks-staten-island-manhunt/), but **no evidence was found** of Santander customers being physically mailed counterfeit bank cards. The available sources describe criminals using number-spoofing for phishing or cloning cards for ATM withdrawals, not a scheme involving sending unsolicited physical cards. Consequently, the specific mechanism claimed could not be verified.